      "claim": "The belief that flies arise from rotting meat is an example of the theory of spontaneous generation.",
      "evidence": "Historical texts (Aristotle, 17th‑century naturalists) describe spontaneous generation as life emerging from non‑living matter such as flies from meat.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Francesco Redi’s 1668 meat‑jar experiment disproved spontaneous generation for flies by showing maggots only appeared when adult flies could lay eggs on the meat.",
      "evidence": "Redi covered some jars with gauze; maggots appeared only in uncovered jars, demonstrating flies come from eggs, not the meat itself (primary source: Redi, *Esperienze Intorno alla Generazione de’ Vermi* 1668).",
      "weight": 0.4,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Subsequent experiments (Spallanzani, Pasteur) continued this line of inquiry, using controlled designs to refute spontaneous generation for microorganisms as well.",
      "evidence": "Spallanzani’s sealed flask experiments (1765) and Pasteur’s swan‑neck flasks (1859) showed that no life appears without an external source, reinforcing the experimental approach.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Therefore the idea was disproved primarily because of continued experimentation, not because of the invention of the microscope, the discovery of the atom, or surgical advances.",
      "evidence": "Historical analyses (e.g., Porter, *The Greatest Benefit to Mankind*) attribute the downfall of spontaneous generation to a series of controlled experiments, while the microscope was merely a tool and not essential for Redi’s key result.",
